{{Politics of Okaiken}}

The {{Nihongo|'''Cabinet of Okaiken'''|内閣|Naikaku}} is the supreme executive organ of the [[Government of Okaiken]]. It is headed by the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]], who is designated by the [[National Diet of Okaiken]] and formally invested by the [[Emperor of Okaiken]], together with up to seventeen [[Ministers of State (Okaiken)|ministers of state]]. The contemporary executive, the [[Ikazaki Cabinet]], was formally installed on 20 May 2026 in the wake of the [[Okaiken general election, 2026|May 2026 general election]].

The governing administration represents a left-leaning parliamentary coalition comprising the [[Sanese Communist Party]] (SCP), the [[Okaiken Socialist Party]] (OSP), and the [[Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Social Democratic Party]] (SDP), supported on confidence and supply by independent island representative [[Hayashi Akihiro]]. Operating with a working majority of 251 seats in the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken]], the Cabinet functions under the binding Yoshida Threshold, which codifies that at least 40 percent of all ministerial portfolios must be held by women. The administration succeeded the collapsed [[Honda-Nakayama Cabinet]], following systemic political tremors surrounding the [[Nakayama Station dragging incident]] and the nationwide [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]].

==Constitutional appointment and tenure==

Under Chapter V of the [[Constitution of Okaiken]], the Prime Minister is selected from among the members of the National Diet by parliamentary resolution. Upon formal appointment by the Emperor at the [[Imperial Palace of Okami]], the Prime Minister appoints the Ministers of State, who must all be civilians and of whom a statutory majority must hold seats in either chamber of the Diet. Under the Cabinet Law, the baseline ministerial complement is fourteen, with statutory authority to expand to seventeen portfolios during complex coalitions or national crises.

Cabinet ministers enjoy constitutional immunity from prosecution during their tenure unless the Prime Minister grants formal consent. The Cabinet is collectively responsible to the Diet and must resign in its entirety under three constitutional triggers: upon the passage of a motion of no confidence or the defeat of a confidence motion in the House of Commoners unless the chamber is dissolved within ten days, upon the initial convocation of the Diet following a general election, or upon the vacancy or resignation of the Prime Minister. While an outgoing Cabinet resigns, it remains in caretaker status until the investiture of a successor premiership.

==Executive powers and authority==

Constitutional executive power is formally vested in the Cabinet as a collegial organ rather than in the Crown or individual bureaucratic departments. The Emperor acts solely upon the binding advice and consent of the Cabinet in ceremonial functions, including the promulgation of laws and treaties, the convocation of the Diet, the dissolution of the House of Commoners, the proclamation of elections, and the conferral of state honours.

The explicit constitutional authorities exercised directly by the Cabinet encompass the administration of the civil service, the conduct of foreign affairs, the conclusion of international treaties subject to Diet ratification, the drafting and submission of the national budget, and the enactment of Cabinet orders to execute statutory law. The Cabinet also holds the power of general and special amnesty and nominates the Chief Justice and Associate Justices of the [[Supreme Court of Okaiken]]. All statutory enactments and Cabinet orders require the signature of the competent Minister of State and the countersignature of the Prime Minister.

==Relations with prefectural and metropolitan governments==

The Cabinet maintains a complex constitutional relationship with Okaiken's forty-eight subnational jurisdictions, consisting of forty-seven prefectures and the unique capital jurisdiction of [[Okami Metropolis]]. Under the Local Autonomy Act, prefectures possess constitutionally protected self-governing authority, led by directly elected governors and unicameral prefectural assemblies. The national Cabinet does not possess arbitrary removal powers over local chief executives, necessitating institutional coordination across all major portfolios.

Central intergovernmental coordination is formally anchored within the [[Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications]] through the Local Finance Bureau and the [[Local Allocation Tax]] mechanism, which redistributes national revenues to ensure baseline public service delivery across economically disparate regions. Subnational viewpoints are formally channeled to the Cabinet through the National Governors' Association, the Japan Association of City Mayors, and the National Association of Towns and Villages. Regular liaison conferences between Cabinet ministers and prefectural executives are convened at the Prime Minister's Official Residence to negotiate national infrastructure allocations, public healthcare standards, and disaster preparedness frameworks.

Tensions between the national Cabinet and regional authorities frequently arise over the structural phenomenon known as the Okami Magnet, whereby corporate capital and youth demographics concentrate heavily within Okami Metropolis at the expense of outer prefectures such as [[Miyagi Prefecture]], [[Iwate Prefecture]], and island territories. The [[Okami Metropolitan Government]], possessing an autonomous municipal budget comparable to mid-sized sovereign states, exercises significant leverage over national policy implementation. While the [[National Public Safety Commission]] sets nationwide policing standards, operational policing in the capital is directed by the [[Okami Metropolitan Police Department]] under the supervision of the Okami Metropolitan Public Safety Commission, requiring continuous statutory cooperation between central ministers and the Governor of Okami Metropolis.

==Current Cabinet==

The 110th Cabinet of Okaiken, formed on 20 May 2026 and led by Prime Minister [[Kousuke Ikazaki]], exercises executive authority across seventeen ministries, agencies, and central coordinating commissions.

